<p>Thank you for the critique. I'll go over the minor issues you mentioned first:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+RE:&nbsp;Line-by-line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I clench every time I see a collapsible in the containment procedures. 99% of the time, it's not really necessary, in-universe because these are electronic documents and presumably they would updated by the Foundation in a manner befitting that, and out-of-universe because unless you've got some extremely complex story that demands something like this (and in this case, I don't think you do), this serves as filler. We should all be focusing more these days not necessarily on shortening our narratives, but rather cutting parts that are unnecessary. I can invest myself in a long article, but if it's weighed down by filler I'm going to check out.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is all a fair point, and something that I felt uncertain about even as I was writing this. I'm going to go ahead and remove it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This article has huge problems with weird and pointless redaction. What exactly is the purpose of allowing a reader to know what decade something is occurring in but not the precise year?<br />
&#8230;.<br />
And why are we allowed to know that there are between 100-199 instances, but not the specific number?<br />
&#8230;.<br />
Again; why?<br />
&#8230;<br />
Why on earth is that classified?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, fair criticisms. I guess I've gotten so used to other articles doing these kinds of redactions, I didn't stop and think that it might annoy some people. I'll go ahead and undo these as well.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is a little familiar for clinical tone. &quot;Diatribe&quot; has connotations of a bitter and somewhat vitriolic statement, and thus carries with it subjective value judgments. As do the terms &quot;minutiae&quot; and &quot;limited knowledge.&quot; The Foundation feels like it's weighing in on the quality of the videos here when there's really no call to do that.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Clinical tone is something that I've constantly struggled with, on and off the site. This I've made no secret, and it's part of the reason why this article took so long to finally be put up. I might get around to re-wording this particular bit later to be more objective.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The term &quot;extradimensional&quot; gets abused constantly around here (I too am an offender in that regard), but really, what does it mean, and why is it assumed that this thing is &quot;extradimensional&quot;? Do we really know enough about it to make that supposition?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The crystal person is labeled as &quot;extradimensional&quot; because it can't be seen on the purely visual spectrum, only when it's being recorded by a camera.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>There is no reason to strike peoples' names like this. From the rest of the article, I know this person's state of residence, the number of letters in their hometown, and the circumstances of their death. I could likely find who this is with a ten minute Google search. Either the reader is cleared to know the name, or they shouldn't be, in which case, why leave a trail of easily-followable breadcrumbs?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>When it comes to explaining choices such as these, there are the options of <a href="http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/WatsonianVersusDoylist">Watsonian and Doylist</a>. For the sake of this reply, I'll go with the Doylist (out-of-universe) approach.</p>
<p>&#8230;</p>
<p>I couldn't think of a good last name for him. Bit of a lame answer, I know but ¯\_(ツ)_/¯ it's the truth.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>See my previous complaints on why someone managing an electronic document wouldn't leave strikethroughs and such all over the place.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Once again, fair point. I'll probably change the strikethrough into a footnote or some such.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This seems like a weird bit to throw in, and I'm not sure why it's here. The use of non-language symbols in this context seems like a bad idea for organizational consistency.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I explained this up a few replies, but I'll add that it's something that I think gives my article (and any future ones I make involving DIAMOND) that extra bit of uniqueness.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>As far as the characterization of Simon and the entity go, I will concede that I didn't give as good of characterizations to either of them as I could've. I gave a reasoning for why the androgynous crystal person was so interested in him in an earlier version of the addendum, but I truncated it, and now all that's left is the implicitness in the logs. Something for me to think about in the future&#8230;</p>
<p>Again, I do want to thank you for your words, even if I kinda had to ask you to do so :P</p>

							</div>

								
					
